  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nExcessively hot temperatures simmered over the forecast area this \nafternoon as a high pressure system dominated the western half of \nthe CONUS. By noon, temperatures had reached the low 90s with \ndewpoints spanning the 70s. Heat indices ranged from 99 to 105.  \n\nBut relief is in sight! Surface analysis indicates a cold front \ncurrently draped from north central Nebraska to southern Minnesota, \nassociated with a midlevel low moving into the Great Lakes, will \nmove southeast across eastern Nebraska and western Iowa this \nafternoon and evening. Instability will pool ahead of the boundary, \nsetting the stage for deep moist convective updrafts to develop. \nHowever sustained strong storms may be limited by strongest shear \nvectors lingering just behind the front. While storms likely won't \nsurvive long enough to produce large hail, they could fall down in a \nhurry, creating the potential for strong, damaging wind gusts. This \ncan be verified by noting the 1400-1600 J/kg of DCAPE building over \nnortheast Nebraska.\n\nIn addition to thunderstorms, northerly winds behind the front could \nbring down some near-surface smoke from Canadian wild fires. While \nit's not expected to be nearly as impactful as it was near the Great \nLakes recently, it may be noticeable, especially across the northern \nhalf of the CWA.\n\nCAMs hint at another line of elevated showers and storms developing \nbehind the surface front late tonight and into early Tuesday \nmorning, as the LLJ ramps up. However these storms are even less \nlikely to become strong or severe, as the front scours out remaining \ninstability with it's passage.\n\nTuesday through Friday... \n\nStorm coverage will gradually dissipate through the morning, \nTuesday, as the line moves south. Temperatures will finally take it \ndown a notch, behind the passing front Tuesday afternoon. Highs are \nexpected to be within a few degrees of \"normal\", reaching the \nmid and upper 80s by the afternoon. \n\nA much more noticeable change in temperatures arrives Wednesday and \ncontinues through Friday. Highs will generally reach the upper 70s \nand lower 80s Wednesday and Friday, with a few locations only \ntopping out in the mid to upper 70s Thursday!\n\nA pair of quick-hitting shortwaves will aide in the push of cooler \nair Tuesday night/Wednesday and Thursday/Friday morning. Not only \nwill these bring cooler temperatures, but also some much needed \nwetting rains with showers and thunderstorms. WPC highlights \nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa with a Slight Risk for \nexcessive rainfall Thursday, where deep moisture and training storms \nmay be possible.  \n\nSaturday and Beyond...\n\nUnfortunately, longer range models continue to hint at the potential \nfor the ridge to rebound this weekend and into next week, bringing a \nresurgence of heat. A few shortwaves rippling around the ridge could \nbring some mild relief with cloud cover and low precip chances. But \noverall, CPC outlooks maintain above normal temperatures are likely \nto stick around through at least early August.",
